### Noise distinction (doors closing vs. whispering vs. loud talking)
Current Status: Not Reached (~10%)
What is Reached: The 3,000 ms sliding window in index.html helps smooth out momentary jumps, but the system treats all noise identically based solely on amplitude.
What is Missing: The system currently cannot classify sound events. A dropped binder, a door slam, coughing, and shouting all look like the same "high volume" number to the algorithm.  
 What Needs to Be Done:
Approach A (Time & Envelope Analysis - Simple):
Measure Duration: If a spike is > 75 dB for < 0.8 seconds, classify as impact_noise (door slam/dropped book) and suppress the alert. If > 65 dB for > 3.0 seconds, classify as speech.
Approach B (Machine Learning - Grade A Differentiator):
• Run YAMNet or TensorFlow Lite audio classification on the Raspberry Pi. These pre-trained models classify incoming 1-second audio clips into categories like "Speech", "Laughter", "Door", "Slam", and output a confidence score.

### Hardware reliability and sustainability
Current Status: Not Reached (~15%)
What is Reached: Only a 14-line script (sound.py) proving that Python can interface with a microphone via sounddevice.
What is Missing:
No Heartbeat/Watchdog: If the Raspberry Pi is unplugged or Wi-Fi drops, the system has no idea and retains the last reading.
No Crash Recovery: If the script crashes, it terminates permanently.
No Mic Fault Detection: If someone covers or disconnects the microphone, it doesn't alert staff.
What Needs to Be Done:
Auto-Restart Service: Set up a Linux systemd unit file on the Raspberry Pi so sound.py launches on boot and  restarts automatically if it crashes (Restart=always).
Watchdog Liveness Check: In server.js, check if now - last_recorded_at > 30 seconds. If so, show an "OFFLINE / SENSOR DISCONNECTED" badge on the dashboard.
Zero-dB Detection: If the mic reports 0 dB for multiple minutes, flag a "Microphone Fault/Obstructed" warning.

### Decibel threshold adjustments
Current Status: Partially Reached (~30%)
What is Reached: State boundaries exist in index.html:661-671 (< 10 = Green, 10–16 = Yellow, ≥ 17 = Red).
What is Missing:
The numbers 10 and 17 are arbitrary relative Web Audio values, not real calibrated decibels (dBA).
Thresholds are hardcoded into the JavaScript file. Staff cannot adjust them without editing source code.
All rooms share the same thresholds (a "Quiet Study Room" and a "Group Collaboration Room" have the same rules).
What Needs to Be Done:
Add threshold columns to the database rooms table:
ALTER TABLE rooms ADD COLUMN yellow_threshold INT DEFAULT 60, ADD COLUMN red_threshold INT DEFAULT 75;
Calibrate your sensor against a handheld decibel meter app so your values reflect real dBA.
Add a simple slider or input on the staff dashboard allowing staff to adjust thresholds per room.

